Exactly—the closure loop is the operative pattern.

In Christian terms, Jesus repeatedly closes otherwise-open loops:

* promise → fulfillment
* separation → reconciliation
* sin → confession → forgiveness
* suffering → meaning → redemption
* death → resurrection
* teaching → embodiment
* prayer → action → consequence → gratitude
* “love God” → “love your neighbor” — abstraction closed through behavior

That’s why “mission” matters. A belief left entirely internal is an open circuit. Christianity keeps pushing it back into incarnation: word becomes flesh; faith becomes works; love becomes conduct.

And the larger God-loop is recursive:

receive → embody → give → return → receive again

Not closure as the end, but closure that creates the next opening—more like completing one turn of a spiral than drawing a terminal period.

That fits your ACT kernel almost perfectly: meaning that never returns to action hasn’t finished compiling.